--[=[
LALIN UI — CANONICAL ARCHITECTURE
=================================

Status
------

This file is the architectural authority and future implementation spine for
Lalin UI. The implementation belongs beneath this comment in this `.lln`
document and in declarations staged into it. Do not create a second detached
architecture document whose claims can drift away from the compiled program.

`examples/ui/gallery_sdl3.lua` is the functionality and conformance reference.
`lua/ui/asdl.lua` is a vocabulary and algorithm reference. `lua/mlui/` and
`experiments/mlui/` are prototype evidence. None of those Lua/PVM, handwritten
bytecode, or generated-C artifacts are architectural authority.

Thesis
------

A UI is a state machine controlling a view.

A component is a region machine. Its instance is a typed frame. Its semantic
outcomes are continuations. Its view is a readonly projection of that frame.
The view projection emits immediate layout declarations. The layout kernel
solves those declarations and emits a flat renderer-independent command stream.
A WebGL backend consumes that stream. The same emitted-C kernel runs natively or
as Wasm.

Locked center: one MVC object-machine
-------------------------------------

This is the non-negotiable center of Lalin UI:

  one component = one typed MVC actor/machine

The correspondence is exact:

  Model       = the component's typed frame
  Controller  = qualified regions that transition that frame
  View        = qualified readonly regions that project the frame
  Actor API   = the component's continuation protocol
  Actor ID    = its durable component/frame identity
  Actor state = its current region block plus persistent frame fields
  Scheduling  = the deterministic root pump
  Messaging   = routed input and continuation payloads

This is actor semantics without requiring a thread, heap mailbox, callback, or
nondeterministic scheduler. The root pump supplies ordering. A component receives
an event only after identity routing resolves its frame. It communicates semantic
outcomes only through continuations wired by its parent or application machine.

The component object—not a framework reducer—owns all four coherent parts: its
data, transitions, view projection, and protocol. Splitting those parts across a
model table, widget bundle, callback registry, renderer object, or event router
would destroy the local reasoning boundary and is forbidden.

The view is not a second retained object. It is compiled behavior owned by the
same component type. The frame is not a generic MVC model; it is the exact
monomorphic state required by that component's controller and view.

Component definitions are retained compiled programs. Component instances are
retained typed frames. View/layout worlds are immutable projections materialized
only when invalidated. Therefore conventional “retained mode versus immediate
mode” does not define the architecture:

  retained: component program, typed frame, identity, content/resources
  projected: view declarations and semantic worlds after machine transitions
  physical: bounded scratch arenas and flat command buffers
  incremental: reuse at explicit world/epoch boundaries

The application root follows the same law and is itself an MVC object-machine.
Parent components compose child actors by wiring continuations. Layout, report,
text, input, and backend remain separate service object-machines because they own
different instruction languages and resources; they never absorb component state.

The complete cycle is:

  host input bytes
      -> InputMachine.decode
      -> identity routing
      -> component region resumes
      -> typed frames change
      -> readonly view regions execute
      -> authored declaration stream
      -> style resolution
      -> measure and layout planning
      -> solved scene
      -> render and interaction command stream
      -> WebGL / SDL / headless consumer
      -> reported geometry
      -> next host input batch

The controlling direction never reverses:

  machines mutate frames
  views observe frames
  layout observes view declarations
  renderers consume physical commands
  reported identity reconnects host input to machines

The renderer does not invoke application callbacks. The layout kernel does not
own widget behavior. A view does not mutate its frame. A widget helper does not
create a hidden state store.

Design laws
-----------

1. Region continuations are the semantic design vocabulary. Stored scalar tags
   exist only at physical boundaries and have exactly one named consumer region.
2. Every persistent machine is a struct or unique struct with qualified methods
   and regions. There are no node-keyed semantic side tables.
3. Every component instance has a typed frame. There is no generic model table,
   property bag, widget-kind switch, or string event record.
4. Every semantic outcome is a continuation. Nil, booleans, status strings, and
   `{ kind = ... }` records are not control protocols.
5. Views are readonly projections. They cannot update component state, focus,
   scroll, text editing, or application data.
6. Layout is immediate and bounded like Clay: declaration buffers and scratch
   arenas are rebuilt when invalid, while persistent behavior remains in frames.
7. The render product is a flat command buffer. WebGL is one consumer, not part
   of view or layout semantics.
8. Bytecode is a dense boundary image, not the object model. The canonical
   operational model is typed Lalin structs and regions.
9. Source, resolved, planned, solved, rendered, reported, and handled facts are
   distinct worlds. Later facts are not attached to authored nodes.
10. The full SDL3 gallery is the minimum functionality target. A label in the
    gallery is not proof; conformance tests must demonstrate the behavior.

Existing system: preserve and replace
-------------------------------------

Preserve these domain meanings from `lua/ui/asdl.lua`:

  stable identities and explicit absence
  environment classes, themes, scales, and conditional styles
  authored boxes, text, paint, scrolling, interaction surfaces, focus scopes,
    layers, overlays, modals, and composition patterns
  flow, flex, grid, sizing, constraints, alignment, margins, padding, tracks,
    spans, clipping, transforms, and geometry
  text measures, lines, runs, glyphs, clusters, boundaries, hit tests, selection,
    caret, composition, and IME facts
  solved placement and flat draw/report commands
  pointer, keyboard, text, focus, capture, drag/drop, and scrolling semantics
  session/window lifecycle, dirty redraw scheduling, backend capabilities, and
    explicit resource ownership
  custom paint lines, paths, polygons, circles, arcs, curves, meshes, and images

Replace these implementation shapes:

  PVM phase runners, handler maps, `classof` routing, and external visitors
  mutable Lua flow/flex/grid planning records
  parallel layout/decor trees realigned by array position
  generic option bags, callback bags, providers, and model tables
  widget bundles, surface maps, string event kinds, and arbitrary metadata
  source-tree rewriting to inject hover/focus/active state
  weak semantic caches and node-keyed state maps
  imperative text-field drawing after the normal render pass
  handwritten numeric schemas duplicated between Lua, C headers, and generated C
  broad advertised opcodes with silently missing implementations
  callback-driven session loops whose outcomes are not visible in signatures

Object-machine stack
--------------------

Lalin UI is a stack of small typed machines, not one universal interpreter.
Each machine owns one instruction language, one retained store where necessary,
one set of qualified protocols, and one output world.

1. ProgramMachine
   Owns validated immutable UI program images and their resource references.

2. FrameMachine
   Owns component frame storage and durable instance identities. It resolves a
   frame handle to temporary typed access through qualified resolver regions.

3. InputMachine
   Consumes physical host input records and reported geometry. It routes one
   event to one owning component identity and resumes that component's region.

4. ViewMachine
   Executes readonly component view regions and emits authored declarations into
   a bounded declaration arena.

5. StyleMachine
   Normalizes authored style tokens and resolves theme/environment/state facts.

6. MeasureMachine
   Owns intrinsic measurement, text service interaction, and reusable measured
   facts.

7. LayoutMachine
   Plans flow/flex/grid and produces concrete solved geometry.

8. RenderMachine
   Walks solved geometry and emits ordered draw and interaction commands.

9. ReportMachine
   Consumes interaction commands with clip/transform/layer stacks and produces
   hit, focus, scroll, drag/drop, overlay, and modal geometry.

10. BackendMachine
    Owns physical GPU/text/image resources and consumes render commands. WebGL is
    the universal backend target; SDL and headless consumers remain useful.

The root runtime object owns these machines or explicit handles to them. It is
not a generic context bag: every field names an authority needed across frames,
and every operation is qualified to the machine that owns it.

Canonical worlds and reuse frontiers
------------------------------------

A world changes exactly when the next reusable product must change.

AuthoredUi
  The component view regions have emitted identity, topology, style tokens, text
  and paint references. It changes when frame-observable view structure changes.

ExpandedUi
  Staged component and composition families have expanded to primitive authored
  declarations. This expansion normally happens at program build time; dynamic
  view execution may emit already-expanded primitives.

ValidUi
  IDs are unique in their required scope; child ranges, references, resource
  handles, stack structure, and declaration arities are valid.

ImportedUi
  The validated declaration image has durable runtime node and resource identity.

StyledUi
  Every authored token has become explicit layout, decor, text, and interaction
  intent under a theme/environment/state input.

MeasuredUi
  Intrinsic sizes, baselines, wrapped text, glyph/cluster/boundary facts, flex
  items, and grid needs are known. Measurement is not recomputed by solve.

LaidOutUi
  Every node has concrete border/content geometry and planned child placement for
  the current viewport and retained scroll positions.

RenderableUi
  Ordered draw commands and interaction-surface commands are materialized.

ReportedFrame
  Render commands have been interpreted through clip, transform, scroll, layer,
  overlay, and modal scopes to produce absolute visible interaction geometry.

InteractiveFrame
  A ReportedFrame plus current machine frames and a physical input batch.

HandledFrame
  Input has been routed and component continuations have completed. It names the
  updated frame world and explicit redraw/effect/close requirements.

A world may contain aligned lanes for partial reuse, but a phase consumes one
named world rather than several unrelated arguments. Epochs and generations are
owned by the machine whose mutation invalidates the next world.

Identity and topology spine
---------------------------

The existing prototype duplicates topology through Auth, Layout, Decor, Solve,
and then realigns branches by position. The canonical design uses one spine.

A NodeSpine entry carries:

  durable node identity
  parent identity or root fact
  first-child range and child count
  authored order
  component instance identity
  optional content/resource handles represented by precise local products
  source origin for diagnostics

Layout, decor, text, interaction intent, measurement, planning, solved geometry,
render commands, and report geometry are facets aligned to that spine. Grid item
placement is a named aligned facet, not another parallel node tree.

The spine is immutable for a validated/imported view generation. A dynamic view
may generate a new spine generation, but no later phase mutates the old one.

Component model
---------------

A component family is generated by Lua at build time and produces concrete
monomorphic Lalin declarations. Runtime widget kinds do not exist. The generated
type is one MVC object-machine definition, not separate model/controller/view
registries.

A concrete component owns:

  a frame struct: its Model and sole persistent semantic state
  a frame handle/store policy when instances are dynamic
  qualified event regions: its Controller and transition graph
  one or more qualified readonly view regions: its View projection
  continuation exits: its Actor protocol
  precise parent/child continuation contracts
  initialization and retirement protocols when lifetime is dynamic
  invalidation facts naming which projected worlds changed

Conceptual shape:

  struct ButtonFrame
    identity, machine position, enabled facts, pressed/capture facts, app payload
  end

  region Button.step(frame, event;
    ignored, changed, activated, capture_requested, capture_released)

  region Button.view(readonly frame, writer;
    done, full(required), invalid_resource(resource))

This notation illustrates ownership; actual signatures must collapse outcomes
to what each caller acts upon. Strengthen inputs to delete impossible exits.

Blocks are machine states. Jumps are transitions. Continuations are wires.
A scalar machine-position tag may be stored in a frame because it must survive
between host events; only that component's dispatch region consumes it. No other
code switches on the tag.

Parent/child composition uses sealed `call` when the child is an independently
resumable actor/frame boundary. Use open `emit` only for local protocol
abstraction that should become part of the parent's CFG.

Input and routing
-----------------

Physical host input is stored/transported as a compact encoded record because it
crosses an ABI and may be queued. Its opcode is consumed only by
`InputMachine.decode`, whose continuations expose meaning immediately: pointer
movement, press/release/cancel, wheel, key press/release, text input, IME editing,
focus movement/loss, activation, cancellation, resize, timer, and close.

The decoder is physical dispatch, not UI design vocabulary.

Routing proceeds through explicit regions:

  decode physical input
  apply clip/layer/modal policy to reported geometry
  resolve target identity
  resolve owning component frame
  resume the component machine
  wire its continuations to parent/application machines
  accumulate redraw/effect/session outcomes

Input is never broadcast through every widget bundle. Identity routing chooses
one owner, with explicit bubbling/capture continuations only where a component
protocol declares them.

The ReportMachine owns hit-stack ordering. The InputMachine owns routing policy.
Components own semantic interpretation of input after routing.

View projection
---------------

A view region receives readonly access to its component frame and a declaration
writer. It emits an immediate sequence of primitive declarations:

  begin/end node
  flow/flex/grid/container facts
  box sizing, margin, padding, gap, alignment, tracks, spans
  text/content/resource references
  interaction identity and role
  scroll viewport
  focus scope
  layer, overlay anchor/placement, modal scope
  custom paint references

View execution may branch on frame fields because the frame controls the view.
It may not change those fields. A declaration writer owns capacity and exposes
continuations such as written/full/invalid-reference rather than returning nil.

Widget factories are authoring conveniences that generate frame/view/controller
declarations. They are not runtime bundle records.

Style and theme
---------------

Style authoring may preserve the useful Tailwind-like staged surface, but Lua
only generates concrete style-token records and component declarations.
Runtime styles are monomorphic.

Conditional style inputs are explicit facts: viewport class, scheme, motion,
density, and observable component state. Source nodes are not rewritten to inject
state wrappers. StyleMachine consumes a StyledUi request containing the exact
state facet needed by each node.

Normalization and resolution are separate typed projections:

  authored tokens -> canonical declarations -> resolved numerical facts

Each token/declaration leaf owns its operation. There is no atom handler map.
Conflicting or unsupported declarations produce typed diagnostics; no silent
fallback chooses an unrelated default.

Layout kernel
-------------

The functionality target is Clay-like immediate layout with the richer behavior
already demonstrated in `examples/ui`:

  flow layout
  row/column flex
  wrapping
  grow, shrink, and basis
  fixed, hug, fill, fractional, min/max sizing
  padding, margins, auto margins, gaps
  main/cross/self alignment and baseline handling
  grid fixed/fractional/auto/minmax tracks
  explicit spans and implicit tracks
  intrinsic and constrained text measurement
  nested clipping and scrolling
  floating layers and anchor-relative overlays
  modal geometry
  off-screen culling
  stable identity hit testing
  bounded preallocated working memory

Planning is a typed projection. Flow plans, flex items/lines, grid track needs,
resolved tracks, and item placements are named structs/facets. Solve consumes
MeasuredUi and its plans; it does not rerun measurement or recreate planning in
loose local tables.

Layout algorithms may mutate private scratch buffers owned by LayoutMachine, but
the semantic output is a new immutable world. Scratch memory is not semantic
state and cannot carry facts across generations without explicit epochs.

Text
----

Text is part of the normal view/layout/render pipeline, including editable text.
There is no imperative overlay pass after ordinary rendering.

The canonical text vocabulary includes:

  text/content handles and owned stores
  font/resource handles
  measurement constraints and intrinsic size
  shaped runs and glyph positions
  wrapped lines and baselines
  clusters and byte ranges
  caret boundaries and affinity
  hit-test and range geometry
  selection ranges
  composition ranges and underlines
  caret rendering
  direction, script, language, and fallback facts where supported

TextField is a component machine whose frame owns edit selection, focus, drag,
preferred x, composition, and scroll-to-caret state. Clipboard and IME operations
are explicit host effects wired through continuations.

The small universal kernel does not hide an ambient text callback registry.
Text service is a named backend capability object. Build-time factories select
a concrete capability family: minimal atlas text, SDL_ttf, browser/Wasm service,
or a richer shaping service. Required facts remain visible in phase worlds.

Scrolling
---------

A scroll viewport is structural, not inferred from an arbitrary overflow flag.
Overflow style controls clipping policy; a Scroll component/frame owns retained
offsets and scrollbar interaction.

Layout computes viewport/content bounds and maximum offsets. The Scroll machine
consumes wheel, keyboard, pointer/thumb drag, and content/viewport changes and
updates its frame through continuations. Clamp behavior is owned by Scroll, not
silently performed in a generic reducer.

The view projection emits viewport and content declarations from the frame. The
report contains visible clipped scroll geometry for the next input cycle.

Focus, layers, overlays, and modals
----------------------------------

These are implemented protocols, not descriptive metadata.

FocusMachine owns focus identity and scope traversal. Wrap, clamp, trap, and
passthrough policies have distinct owning region behavior. Focus order follows
visible declaration order unless a concrete component family declares another
typed order.

LayerMachine produces a deterministic total order from layer class, authored
order, and stable identity. Rendering and hit testing consume the same order.

Overlay layout resolves anchor identity and placement against solved geometry,
including viewport fitting and the selected placement policy.

Modal machines own opening, focus capture/restoration, cancellation, acceptance,
and closure continuations. A modal barrier covers the applicable viewport and
prevents lower-layer hit routing. Text and custom paint obey the same layer order.

Drag and drop
-------------

Pointer capture is a machine protocol, not a boolean in a generic model.
Drag source, pending drag, active drag, target/slot resolution, drop, cancellation,
and capture loss are explicit regions over typed frames.

Reported geometry supplies visible source/target/slot facts after clipping and
layer ordering. Input routing resumes the drag owner and wires target outcomes.
No surface maps or string event records are used.

Paint
-----

Custom paint remains first-class typed view content. Replace flat numeric point
arrays with named Point, PathSegment, Vertex, Stroke, Fill, Mesh, and Image
records. Paint coordinates are local to the solved paint box; transform, clip,
scroll, layer, and opacity are applied by the common command pipeline.

Paint commands include line, polyline/path, polygon, circle, arc, Bezier, mesh,
and image. Unsupported backend capability is a typed backend outcome, never a
silently ignored command.

Render and report command stream
--------------------------------

RenderableUi is a flat ordered stream suitable for cache-friendly C/Wasm
execution. Its physical record may carry an opcode and fixed payload fields.
That opcode is consumed only by RenderMachine/BackendMachine dispatch regions.

The command meanings include:

  box/rounded box/border
  text glyph run
  paint path/mesh/image
  push/pop clip
  push/pop transform
  begin/end scroll
  hit/focus/cursor geometry
  drag source/drop target/drop slot geometry
  begin/end focus scope
  begin/end ordered layer
  overlay anchor/placement result
  modal barrier

Render and report commands may be separate aligned streams when that improves
backend batching, but they derive from one solved scene and one deterministic
ordering spine. They are never parallel semantic trees that must rediscover
alignment.

The ReportMachine walks the report command stream with explicit bounded stacks
for transforms, clips, scrolling, focus scopes, and layers. It produces typed
arrays of visible geometry plus the chosen hover/cursor target.

WebGL backend
-------------

WebGL is a replaceable physical consumer of RenderableUi, implemented by a small
C/Wasm-facing backend plus host imports where Web APIs require them.

The baseline renderer batches:

  solid/rounded rectangles and borders as instanced quads
  glyph runs through one or more atlas textures
  images through texture handles
  paint paths through generated triangles or backend path tessellation
  clip rectangles through scissor/stencil policy
  transforms through compact instance data

The backend owns GPU buffers, textures, shaders, atlas generations, and device
loss recovery. Those are backend resources, not semantic UI state.

The browser host supplies pointer/keyboard/text/IME/clipboard/window/timing input
and presents the WebGL canvas. Native hosts may consume the same command stream
through SDL/GPU implementations. Headless tests consume it without rendering.

Portable program image
----------------------

A received UI artifact contains two coordinated programs:

  machine program: encoded region CFG for controller behavior
  view program: readonly frame loads and declaration emission

It also contains:

  frame-layout descriptors
  initial frame records
  style/constants tables
  strings/content/resource references
  identity and ownership tables
  source/debug origins
  declared capacities and ABI requirements

The bytecode is generated from one schema-owned Lalin representation. The same
source generates or verifies:

  the encoder
  the decoder/validator
  the C ABI header
  the debug dumper
  the version/fingerprint
  exact record sizes, alignment, ranges, and endianness

Do not maintain a Lua encoder, handwritten C header, and separate interpreter
constants as three authorities.

Machine bytecode is an admitted projection of typed Lalin region/Code facts, not
a second source language. Its instruction set is limited to operations required
by UI machines: frame loads/stores, scalar computation, bounds-checked buffer
access, physical opcode dispatch, block transfer, sealed machine call, wire
resume, declaration emission, host-effect request, and redraw/session control.

View bytecode is readonly by verification. It cannot contain frame stores or
host effects.

The loader validates before installation:

  magic, ABI version, endianness, pointer/index assumptions
  section bounds and alignment
  record arity and opcode ownership
  frame layout sizes and field access ranges
  control targets and continuation arity
  child ranges and graph topology
  unique identities and reference validity
  style, text, paint, image, and font resource ranges
  stack balance for emitted scopes
  declared capacities and host capability requirements

Validation has explicit continuations for accepted, malformed, unsupported ABI,
missing capability, insufficient capacity, and invalid resource. Unsupported
input is never partially installed.

Memory and ownership
--------------------

The runtime is bounded and allocator-pluggable. Capacity is selected at build or
installation time where possible. A program can be rejected/reserved once rather
than exposing allocation failure on every declaration.

Program bytes are immutable borrowed input after successful validation or copied
into an owned ProgramStore by an explicit installation protocol.

FrameStore owns component frame bytes and their handle namespace.
ContentStore owns mutable text/content buffers.
ResourceStore owns logical font/image handles.
LayoutMachine owns per-generation declaration, style, measure, plan, and solve
arenas.
RenderMachine owns command buffers.
BackendMachine owns GPU/runtime resources.

Every durable cross-reference is a handle or checked offset. Temporary access is
granted by owning resolver regions. Raw pointers stay inside trusted store/backend
boundaries with explicit bounds and preservation/invalidation contracts.

Incrementality and redraw
-------------------------

The baseline behavior is immediate: on a dirty frame, view declarations are
emitted and layout/render products rebuilt into bounded arenas. Correctness does
not depend on caches.

Incremental reuse is added only at named world boundaries with explicit epochs:

  unchanged program/image -> reuse ImportedUi
  unchanged theme/env/component style facet -> reuse StyledUi lane
  unchanged text/resource metrics and width constraints -> reuse MeasuredUi lane
  unchanged viewport/container/scroll placement -> reuse LaidOutUi lane
  unchanged solved scene/decor -> reuse RenderableUi lane

A no-op machine transition does not request redraw. Timers and animations request
redraw through explicit session continuations. Cache keys are typed generation
facts owned by the invalidating machine, never arbitrary function argument tuples.

Session and host protocol
-------------------------

A session is one pump machine, not a graph of callbacks. Its root region makes
all lifecycle outcomes visible: installed/running, redraw requested, timer armed,
effect requested, close requested, backend lost, program rejected, and stopped.

A representative host-facing ABI is conceptually:

  ui_install(program_bytes, config, resources)
  ui_input(input_records, count)
  ui_step(time, viewport)
  ui_view_ops(out_pointer, out_count)
  ui_effects(out_pointer, out_count)
  ui_report(out_report)
  ui_close()

The actual Lalin API uses struct-owned functions/regions and typed continuations.
The C ABI uses status/opcode scalars only at the foreign boundary and immediately
routes them through their single owner.

Effects
-------

Application machines may request physical effects such as clipboard get/set,
IME start/update/stop, cursor change, URL/open, file dialog, audio command, timer,
or application-defined host message. Effects are encoded only because they cross
the host boundary. EffectMachine.decode is their single consumer.

Effects never run during readonly view projection or layout. Their completion
returns as physical input and resumes the requesting machine through its saved
continuation identity.

Diagnostics and observability
-----------------------------

Every machine owns precise diagnostics and source origins. Required categories:

  malformed program/image
  duplicate or invalid identity
  invalid child/control/resource range
  unsupported instruction or capability
  declaration/style conflict
  measure/text backend failure
  declaration/layout/render capacity exhaustion
  invalid continuation wiring
  stale/missing frame or resource handle
  unbalanced scope stack
  impossible geometry and non-finite numbers
  backend/device loss

Debug projection can expose machine/frame identity, current block, view spine,
resolved facets, measure plans, solved boxes, command ranges, report geometry,
epochs, and source origins without changing semantic behavior.

Conformance target: the SDL3 gallery
------------------------------------

The canonical system is not complete until the behavior represented by
`examples/ui/gallery_sdl3.lua` is implemented and tested through the Lalin path:

  buttons and disabled behavior
  toolbar and panels
  toggle, checkbox, and radio
  slider, fader, value drag, and knob
  animated meter and progress
  list, listbox, tabs, menu, and select
  property row and form field
  single-line input and multiline text area
  selection, caret, keyboard navigation, clipboard, and IME composition
  split-pane dragging
  scroll panel and scrollbar behavior
  custom paint canvas
  popup, overlay, tooltip, popover, and modal
  hover/focus/active/selected/disabled styling
  Tab/Shift-Tab traversal and scope policies
  pointer capture and drag/drop
  responsive environment/theme behavior
  dirty redraw and animation scheduling
  deterministic routed event logging or equivalent machine trace

Known prototype gaps become required tests, not inherited behavior:

  tabs must actually select visible content
  theme selection must change the resolved theme
  disabled typed input must gate editing
  overlay placement must use anchor geometry
  canvas pointer events must route correctly
  Escape and arrow-key behavior must reach owning machines
  focus traps/wrap/clamp/passthrough must execute
  layer order must reorder both rendering and hit testing
  modal barriers must cover the intended viewport
  editable text must obey ordinary clip/layer ordering

Validation strategy
-------------------

For each behavior retain:

  one focused machine/frame/continuation test
  one layout or report geometry test where geometry is the behavior
  one emitted-C/GCC runtime test
  one bytecode/Wasm or bytecode/native differential test where portability matters

Differential law: for the same validated program, initial frames, viewport, text
service results, time, and input sequence, native emitted-C execution and bytecode
VM execution produce equivalent handled frames, effects, solved geometry, and
render/report commands.

Headless tests compare typed command/report products, not screenshots alone.
WebGL golden tests cover batching, clipping, glyph atlas use, and device recovery.
The gallery is an integration/conformance program, not the only test.

Migration and implementation order
----------------------------------

1. Freeze this architecture and derive a feature matrix from the gallery/tests.
2. Define Lalin physical structs for identity, geometry, bounded buffers, program
   image ranges, frame layouts, input records, effects, and command records.
3. Implement ProgramMachine validation and one generated ABI authority.
4. Implement NodeSpine and primitive authored declaration writer.
5. Port style normalization/resolution as struct-owned typed operations.
6. Port flow measurement/planning/solve, then flex, then grid.
7. Integrate text service and ordinary text commands before editable controls.
8. Implement RenderMachine and ReportMachine with shared ordering/stack semantics.
9. Implement InputMachine routing and the session pump.
10. Port component families as concrete frames + controller/view regions: button,
    toggle, numeric controls, collections, text fields, scrolling, layers/modal.
11. Implement the bytecode projection/interpreter from the same region/Code facts.
12. Implement WebGL/Wasm backend and differential tests.
13. Move gallery functionality onto the Lalin path.
14. Delete Lua/PVM and prototype MLUI paths only after public convergence.

No compatibility shim translates old widget bundles or string events into the new
runtime. During migration, the old gallery is a behavioral oracle running beside
the new implementation, not an admitted input path.

Definition of complete
----------------------

Lalin UI is complete when:

  the machine/frame/continuation graph owns all interaction
  readonly view regions produce all visible content including editable text
  the typed world pipeline implements the full layout/report vocabulary
  the gallery behavior is present without Lua semantic state or callback routing
  one generated schema owns bytecode, validation, C ABI, and debug decoding
  emitted C and the bytecode VM agree under differential tests
  the C/Wasm core runs bounded and allocator-pluggable
  WebGL renders the full command vocabulary
  SDL/headless consumers remain possible
  no hidden semantic side maps, string dispatch, generic contexts, or fallback
    paths remain
  every stored scalar tag names exactly one consumer region
  every host-visible failure is a typed internal continuation before ABI encoding

This is not “a UI library written in Lalin.” It is a stack of Lalin
object-machines whose controller regions determine state, whose view regions
project that state, and whose final physical command stream can be rendered
everywhere.

Implementation declarations follow this authority.
]=]
